Describe concurrent actors and declare the atomic steps (@computing-concurrent-state: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Enumerate-interleavings-and-explain-a-lost-update.lingua#r_00BF8E9AE4846D0FF622ECBF5A") { r_0091D2A7EF6FC417B026FF3627
Two people can each read a tally of seven, separately calculate eight and each record eight. Both performed an addition, but the final tally records only one. The difficulty is the relationship between their observations and effects. A concurrent computation has multiple activities whose lifetimes overlap. Their instructions can be interleaved on one processor; simultaneous execution on several processors is a separate implementation possibility.

Reuse [Separate a program from the state of its execution](Separate-a-program-from-the-state-of-its-execution.lingua#r_00215351C844E50F14E1E11C36) and [Preserve process state while separating eligibility from scheduling policy](Preserve-process-state-while-separating-eligibility-from-scheduling-policy.lingua#r_009ADB7C7B16D8EF5718E860FA): a program is not its entire execution state, and retaining an actor's instruction position and local values is necessary when another actor runs. The actors in this block have a shared counter x and a separate program position and temporary value each. They need not be operating-system processes. A thread can share an address space while retaining its own execution state; actual process/thread and language contracts require the selected environment's specification.

Declare the first teaching model precisely. All modeled steps occur in one total order that preserves each actor's program order. A shared read returns the most recent preceding write to that location. One read, one local addition, one write and a later declared atomic lock operation are indivisible steps. Values are mathematical integers without overflow. There are no crashes, memory faults, outside writers or weak-memory effects. This is a sequentially consistent abstract model, not a claim that arbitrary source expressions, Python's interpreter lock, Rust accesses or processor instructions supply these guarantees.

Actor i has a fixed finite sequence of positive increments a_i,j. Its local triple (j,p,l) records the next job index, the next instruction position and a temporary value. A finished actor has exhausted its jobs. A global state contains x, a lock flag when used, and all actor triples. An execution is a sequence of states linked by legal labeled transitions; the scheduler chooses an unfinished actor at each step. A prefix can end before the computation finishes. No probability distribution is implied by listing possible choices.

The earlier [Investigate every crash boundary in a durable update model](Investigate-durable-updates-crashes-and-retries.lingua#r_00E0361A5584392811B997F505) treated one updating actor and interrupted storage. That proof explicitly excluded concurrent writers. The present model excludes crashes and durable storage. Combining them would require a new joint specification and proof: correctness of the two separate models does not establish correctness of their composition. Similarly, splitting state among actors or sending messages can change the coordination problem without eliminating order, delivery or failure questions.

**Read and work:** [Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces](../sources/Operating-Systems-Three-Easy-Pieces.lingua#r_009CB72626232ED2E08E80BFC4), chapter 26's opening account of threads, shared state and the increment problem. Read it alongside the earlier process state lesson. Draw one state with two different local values and one shared value. Identify exactly which step observes or changes which component. Explain why saying that an addition is one line of source code does not establish its atomicity. Translate the tally example into a legal six-step execution before introducing any synchronization.

**Continue:** [Enumerate interleavings and explain a lost update](Enumerate-interleavings-and-explain-a-lost-update.lingua#r_00BF8E9AE4846D0FF622ECBF5A).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
